A pair of indexes is enough here. One starts at the beginning and the other starts at the end. Swap those values, then move both indexes toward the center.

For `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, the first swap gives `[4, 2, 3, 1]`, and the second gives `[4, 3, 2, 1]`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    void reverse(int arr[], int n){
        int left = 0;
        int right = n - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            int temp = arr[left];
            arr[left] = arr[right];
            arr[right] = temp;
            ++left;
            --right;
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each pair is handled once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The input array is changed directly. An empty array and a one-element array need no swaps.

This one uses the library function `std::reverse`. It takes the range from the first element up to, but not including, the position after the last element.

The call changes `arr` itself and returns nothing, which matches the required method. For an array of length zero or one, the range is already finished.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>

class Solution{
public:
    void reverse(int arr[], int n){
        std::reverse(arr, arr + n);
    }
};
```

Dry run for `[7, 1, 6, 3]`: the library swaps the outside values first, then the inside values, producing `[3, 6, 1, 7]`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space for the normal random-access array.

A temporary array makes the index mapping very clear. The value at `arr[i]` belongs at `arr[n - 1 - i]` in the reversed result. After filling the temporary array, copy it back to the original array.

This uses more memory than swapping, but it is easy to check and it still updates the given array rather than returning another one.

```java
class Solution {
    public void reverse(int[] arr, int n) {
        int[] reversed = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            reversed[n - 1 - i] = arr[i];
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i] = reversed[i];
        }
    }
}
```

For `[2, 4, 9]`, the first loop builds `[9, 4, 2]`, and the second loop writes those values into `arr`. A zero-length array also works because both loops simply skip.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the temporary array.

The recursive version swaps the outside pair first, then solves the smaller middle part. The helper uses `left` and `right` indexes, so no new array is needed.

Dry run with `[5, 8, 2]`:
- Swap indexes `0` and `2`: `[2, 8, 5]`
- The indexes meet at `1`, so recursion stops.

```java
class Solution {
    public void reverse(int[] arr, int n) {
        reversePart(arr, 0, n - 1);
    }

    private void reversePart(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return;
        }

        int temp = arr[left];
        arr[left] = arr[right];
        arr[right] = temp;

        reversePart(arr, left + 1, right - 1);
    }
}
```

The array is reversed in place. The recursive calls use `O(n)` stack space, which is the main drawback compared with a loop.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

# Intuition
>Two pointer method ✅

- This code reverses an array using the two-pointer technique. 
- It starts with left at the first element and right at the last element. 
- While left is before right, it swaps the elements at those positions, then moves left one step to the right and right one step to the left. 
- This process continues until both pointers meet or cross each other, resulting in the entire array being reversed without using any extra array.

